      "problem": "Let $c=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $d=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $d$ onto $c.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{c\\cdot c}c \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}}c \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot 5}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}c \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{14}⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
